      "text": "## Big Picture: Ecology's Scale Hierarchy"
    },
    {
      "id": "18-ECOLOGY.md#004",
      "path": [
        "18-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:natural-sciences:18-ecology, git-history:natural-sciences:18-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "natural-sciences/18-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "natural-sciences/18-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:natural-sciences:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "natural-sciences",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "natural-sciences",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "18-ECOLOGY - Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nLEVELS OF ECOLOGICAL ORGANIZATION\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  Individual  →  Population  →  Community  →  Ecosystem  →  Biosphere\n     │               │               │              │             │\n  physiology     demographics    interactions    energy flow   global cycles\n  behavior       growth models   food webs       nutrients     climate\n  adaptation     dispersal       succession      trophic       planetary\n                                                 structure     boundaries"
    },
    {
      "id": "18-ECOLOGY.md#005",
      "path": [
        "18-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:natural-sciences:18-ecology, git-history:natural-sciences:18-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "natural-sciences/18-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "natural-sciences/18-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:natural-sciences:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "natural-sciences",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "natural-sciences",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "18-ECOLOGY - Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "CORE QUESTIONS AT EACH LEVEL:\n  Individual:   How does organism survive/reproduce in its environment?\n  Population:   Why do numbers go up and down? What regulates them?\n  Community:    Who eats whom? What determines which species coexist?\n  Ecosystem:    How does energy flow? How do nutrients cycle?\n  Biosphere:    What are the global consequences of biology?"
    },
    {
      "id": "18-ECOLOGY.md#006",
      "path": [
        "18-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:natural-sciences:18-ecology, git-history:natural-sciences:18-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "natural-sciences/18-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "natural-sciences/18-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:natural-sciences:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "natural-sciences",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "natural-sciences",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "18-ECOLOGY - Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "ECOLOGICAL TIMESCALES:\n  Individual:    seconds → years (behavioral responses, lifespan)\n  Population:    years → centuries (demographic dynamics)\n  Community:     decades → millennia (succession, evolution)\n  Ecosystem:     centuries → Myr (soil formation, biogeochemical change)\n  Biosphere:     Myr → Gyr (atmospheric composition, mass extinctions)\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n```"
    },
